{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit capped at one month's rent","requirement":"A landlord may not demand or receive security above one month's rent plus any first-month rent paid at occupancy; qualifying small landlords may take up to two months' rent.","key_value":"1 month's rent","coverage_conditions":"Security for residential property used as the tenant's dwelling. Landlords who are natural persons (or LLCs of natural persons) owning no more than two rental properties with four units total may take two months' rent.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Two-month limit only for natural persons or LLCs of natural persons owning no more than two rental properties totaling four dwelling units"},"exemptions":"Security collected or demanded before July 1, 2024; advance payment of at least six months' rent where the lease term is six months or longer; agreed alterations for a fee; service members may not be charged the small-landlord two-month amount.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2024-07-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"(c) (1) Except as provided in paragraph (2), (3), or (5), a landlord shall not demand or receive security, however denominated, in an amount or value in excess of an amount equal to one month’s rent, in addition to any rent for the first month paid on or before initial occupancy.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit itemized statement and return within 21 days","requirement":"Within 21 calendar days after the tenant vacates, the landlord must furnish an itemized statement of the security and return any remaining portion, with documentation of deduction charges.","key_value":"21 calendar days","coverage_conditions":"Security for residential property used as the dwelling of the tenant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Receipts and estimates need not be sent if repair and cleaning deductions together do not exceed $125, or if the tenant waived those rights; the subdivision's requirements do not apply when the tenancy ends under CCP § 1161(2), (3), or (4).","penalty":"A landlord who in bad faith fails to comply with this subdivision is not entitled to claim any amount of the security.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"(h) (1) No later than 21 calendar days after the tenant has vacated the premises, but not earlier than the time that either the landlord or the tenant provides a notice to terminate the tenancy under Section 1946 or 1946.1, Section 1161 of the Code of Civil Procedure, or not earlier than 60 calendar days prior to the expiration of a fixed-term lease,","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New-tenant processing fees count as security","requirement":"Any payment, fee, deposit or charge imposed at the beginning of a tenancy to reimburse the landlord's cost of processing a new tenant is 'security' under this section, except as provided in Section 1950.6.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental agreements for residential property used as the tenant's dwelling.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Charges falling within the exception in Section 1950.6.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"“security” means any payment, fee, deposit, or charge, including, but not limited to, any payment, fee, deposit, or charge, except as provided in Section 1950.6, that is imposed at the beginning of the tenancy to be used to reimburse the landlord for costs associated with processing a new tenant","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Higher security charged to service members must be explained and refunded","requirement":"A landlord charging a service member more than the standard security because of credit history or similar factors must give a written explanation before the lease is signed and refund the extra amount after six months of current residency.","key_value":"6 months","coverage_conditions":"Service members, as defined in Section 400 of the Military and Veterans Code, renting residential property in which the service member will reside.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2025-04-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"On or after April 1, 2025, if a landlord or its agent charges a service member who rents residential property in which the service member will reside a higher than standard or advertised security pursuant to paragraph (1) due to the credit history, credit score, housing history, or other factor related to the tenant","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
